# Security Guidelines

**Note:** For error handling patterns and logging, see `.cursor/rules/error-handling.mdc`

## Core Principles
- **Defense in Depth**: Multiple security layers (client + server validation + RLS)
- **Never Trust Client Input**: Always validate and sanitize on the server
- **Fail Securely**: Errors deny access by default
- **Least Privilege**: Grant minimum permissions necessary

## Authentication & Authorization

### Server-Side Auth Check (REQUIRED)
Always verify auth on the server — never trust client-side checks. Canonical gated-action pattern: [`src/app/admin/users/_lib/assert-admin-caller.ts`](../../src/app/admin/users/_lib/assert-admin-caller.ts) (`assertAdminCaller`). See `error-handling.mdc` for error envelope shape.

### Authorization (Resource Ownership)
Verify the caller owns the resource before mutations; RLS is the database layer — app code still checks where needed.

### Server Actions

Auth failures in server actions return typed error envelopes, never throw. Envelope shape and error taxonomy are owned by `error-handling.mdc` § Response Envelopes; [`src/app/admin/users/_lib/assert-admin-caller.ts`](../../src/app/admin/users/_lib/assert-admin-caller.ts) (`assertAdminCaller` and its callers) is the shipped canonical pattern.

**Auth Error Handling:** Never leak user existence (use "Invalid credentials" not "User doesn't exist")

**See `.cursor/rules/error-handling.mdc` for detailed error response patterns.**

## Input Validation

Use zod at the server boundary per [`forms.mdc`](forms.mdc):

- Use `schema.safeParse(input)` at the server boundary; return user-safe messages (never raw `ZodError` JSON to clients)
- Prefer shared validators in route `_lib/` or `src/utils/` when the same shape is used from forms and server actions
- **Patterns:** `z.string().uuid()`, `z.string().url()`, `z.custom()` with project guards, `superRefine` for cross-field rules

### Injection Prevention
- ✅ Supabase parameterizes queries automatically
- ❌ NEVER use raw SQL string concatenation
- ✅ React escapes JSX automatically
- ⚠️ Only use `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` with DOMPurify sanitization

## Supabase Row Level Security (RLS)

Without RLS, anyone with the anon key can read/write ALL data. Enable RLS on every new table — no exceptions.

Policy authoring patterns are owned by `supabase-sql.mdc`; migration workflow by the `create-migration` skill and `supabase.mdc`.

### Secret key (elevated access — EXTREME CAUTION)
- ❌ NEVER use the Supabase secret key in client code
- ✅ ONLY in secure server contexts (API routes, server actions, CLI scripts)
- Use sparingly for admin role mutations (in-app server actions on `/admin/users` and CLI scripts)
- Prefer `SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY` (`sb_secret_...` from Dashboard → API Keys); legacy `service_role` JWT still works during Supabase's migration period

**Reference:** See `.cursor/rules/supabase.mdc` for detailed RLS patterns and migration safety

## Data Protection

### DTOs (Data Transfer Objects)
Never expose raw database records — no `select('*')` or returning full rows. Select explicit fields only. Reference: [`src/app/admin/users/actions.ts`](../../src/app/admin/users/actions.ts) (admin user list field selection).

**Never Expose:** `password_hash`, tokens (`reset_token`, `verification_token`), internal notes, PII (`ip_address`, `user_agent`), payment details, API keys, other users' email/PII

### Environment Variables
```bash
# .env.local (NEVER commit - add to .gitignore)
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://xxx.supabase.co
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=eyJxxx  # Safe with RLS
SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY=sb_secret_xxx            # NEVER expose to client
```

**Rules:** Never commit `.env.local`, `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` for browser-safe data only, secret keys NEVER use `NEXT_PUBLIC_` prefix, rotate secrets quarterly

## Next.js Security

### Auth Proxy (route protection)

Session refresh and auth gating live in `proxy.ts` → `src/supabase/proxy.ts` (`updateSession`); the browser client uses default foreground auto-refresh for Client Components. Public routes are `/`, `/auth/**`, `/terms`, `/privacy`, `/reference`, `/workflow`, and `/api/client-logs` (client log relay — see [ADR-0007](docs/adr/ADR-0007-client-log-relay-unauthenticated.md)); unauthenticated users on protected routes are redirected to `/auth/login` with safe `next` preservation, except when a stray auth `code` search param is present — that case logs a diagnostic and redirects to `/auth/error?source=stray_code` instead. Protected-route server reads use `getDisplayAuthClaims()` (`allowExpired: true`, signature verified, no refresh) — see [ADR-0005](docs/adr/ADR-0005-proxy-as-sole-session-authority.md).

**Reference:** `src/supabase/proxy.ts`, `proxy.ts`

### Open Redirect Prevention

Use the shipped util `src/utils/is-safe-redirect.ts` — never re-implement redirect validation inline. See the `/auth/confirm` route for usage.

### Security Headers

Configure CSP, frame-ancestors (or X-Frame-Options), and HSTS via next.config headers. Start strict; `'unsafe-inline'` / `'unsafe-eval'` are temporary compatibility debt requiring a documented removal plan.

**Reference (shipped pattern):** [`src/utils/security-headers.ts`](../../src/utils/security-headers.ts) wired through [`next.config.ts`](../../next.config.ts) — CSP is report-only by default; enforcing mode is opted into via `CSP_ENFORCE` (see the `// debt:` marker in that file for the nonce upgrade path before enforcement).

**Reference:** [Next.js Security](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/building-your-application/configuring/security-headers)

### Rate Limiting

Auth-adjacent and expensive endpoints need rate limiting. Supabase Auth enforces rate limits on its own endpoints; custom API routes and server actions do not. Implementation choice is per-project (no prescribed library).

### CORS Configuration
Only allow specific origins - never use wildcard on authenticated endpoints.

### SSRF (Server-Side Request Forgery)
Whitelist hostnames for any user-controlled outbound URL — block localhost and private ranges. No shipped outbound-fetch util yet; add one alongside the first feature that needs it (redirect validation lives in [`src/utils/is-safe-redirect.ts`](../../src/utils/is-safe-redirect.ts)).

## Common Vulnerabilities

### Broken Access Control
Always verify auth and ownership before operations — see Authentication & Authorization above and RLS policies in `supabase-sql.mdc`.

## Security Checklists

### Pre-Commit
- [ ] New/changed server inputs validated at the boundary (see `forms.mdc`)
- [ ] Auth checks for protected routes
- [ ] Authorization checks verify ownership
- [ ] State-changing operations use server actions (Next.js origin check) or API routes that explicitly verify origin
- [ ] No sensitive data in responses (DTOs)
- [ ] No secrets in code (use env vars)
- [ ] RLS enabled for new tables
- [ ] Error messages don't leak info
- [ ] Security tests written

### New API Endpoint
1. Input validation at server boundary (Zod when available)
2. Auth check if protected
3. Authorization check (ownership)
4. DTO for response
5. Try/catch with safe errors
6. Security tests

### New Database Table
1. Enable RLS immediately
2. Create policies for SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE
3. Test policies with multiple users
4. Never expose secret key

**See `.cursor/rules/supabase.mdc` for migration and RLS implementation details.**

### Security Testing
- [ ] Test without auth (401)
- [ ] Test with wrong user (403)
- [ ] Test with invalid input (400)
- [ ] Verify can't access others' data
- [ ] No sensitive data in responses

**See `.cursor/rules/testing.mdc` for comprehensive testing patterns.**

